  <h2>Answering questions about data you can't see</h2>
  <p>Most of the data that could answer society's hardest questions sits behind walls that exist for good reasons. Hospital records are private because patients were promised they would be. A publisher's archive is its business, built over decades and worth protecting. A government's statistics carry legal obligations about who may see them and for what. Copying any of it to a central place, even for a good cause, breaks the promises those walls keep, and so most of it is never used for research at all.</p>
  <p>Attribution-based control takes a different route. Instead of moving data to a question, it moves the question to the data, runs it where the data already lives, and returns only the answer, with a record of exactly which sources contributed to it. The owner never hands over a copy, and the researcher never holds one. What crosses the wall is the result both sides agreed to, nothing more, and every step of the exchange is logged so either side can check it later.<sup><a href="#prose">1</a></sup></p>
  <h3>What changes for a researcher</h3>
  <p>For a researcher, the change starts with what they no longer have to do. There is no data-sharing agreement to negotiate for each source, no secure enclave to stand up, and no copy to protect once the work is done. In exchange, they write their question as code that each owner can read before it runs, and they accept that some questions will be declined. A researcher who works this way gives up one thing and gains several:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>They never hold a copy of the data, so there's nothing to leak, lose or misuse after the project ends.</li>
    <li>They can ask questions of many sources at once, where before each source needed its own agreement and its own transfer.</li>
    <li>Every answer arrives with its provenance: which sources contributed, under what terms, and with what code.</li>
    <li>Results can be repeated, because the question and its code are recorded alongside the answer they produced.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The trade is a slower first question. The code has to be reviewed by each owner before it runs, and an owner who has never seen a request before will take longer to approve it than one who has. After that first review, a well-written question can run again and again as the data changes, and a second researcher asking a similar question can often reuse the reviewed code rather than starting over. Over a project's life, most researchers find the time spent on review is less than the time they used to spend on access.</p>
